.TH OBSERVER 1 "2026-10-01" "observer 0.24.5" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
observer \- manage this machine's Observer service and its stored tokens
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B observer
.RI [ command ]
.RI [ argument ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B observer
manages the machine token of the Observer service running on this computer, and
keeps the tokens of the other machines this computer watches.
.PP
To watch the machine you are sitting at you need no token at all: the dashboard
connects through the local socket, and membership of the
.B observer
group is what grants access to it. The token exists only so that
.I another
computer can query this one.
.PP
The service generates its own token the first time it starts and keeps it in the
credential store, so nothing has to be configured after installation. No command
ever takes a token as an argument, which is why no token ever reaches your shell
history:
.B token set
reads it from standard input instead.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B share " [\-\-stdout]"
Print the machine token and the certificate fingerprint, with the context needed
to install them on another computer. Requires privileges, because the credential
store is readable only by the service account and by root.
.IP
With
.B \-\-stdout
it prints the token alone, with no trailing newline, for capture in a shell
variable. The newline matters: a trailing one would become part of the value
and be rejected byte by byte by the constant-time comparison.
.TP
.B rotate\-key
Replace the machine token. The previous one keeps working for 24 hours, so
remote clients are not cut off all at once. The service keeps using the old key
until it is restarted; after that, read the new one with
.B share
and hand it to the machines that watch this one with
.BR "token set" .
Requires privileges.
.TP
.B rotate\-key " \-\-now"
The same, for a token that has
.I leaked.
The previous key is not kept: it stops working at once and is not written back
to the store, so the compromised secret is not left on disk. The command then
tells the
.I running
service to adopt the new store through the local channel, and reports whether
it did \- comparing the moment the service says it read the file with the moment
this command wrote it, so that success is observed rather than assumed. It exits
0 only when the old key is provably accepted nowhere on this machine. Every
computer that watches this one is cut off until
.B token set
is run there with the new token. Requires privileges.
.TP
.B doctor
Report where the credential store is, how well it is protected, what identity
this terminal has, and what a client would see on the local channel. On Linux it
also reports who owns the store file and its directory, by number and, where
/etc/passwd and /etc/group list one, by name, and flags a state that stops the
service from starting: a file that belongs to another account than its
directory, which is what
.B rotate\-key
run as root left behind before 0.24.2. It then prints the two commands that
repair it, handing the file to the owner of the directory with
.B "chown \-h"
and restarting the service. If the store is a symbolic link it says so and
offers no command, because the package leaves a link alone too. It says the
same of a FIFO, a device, a directory or a socket where the store belongs, of a
file over 64 KiB, and of a file with a second name that belongs to another
account than its directory, because a change of owner would change the file
behind the other name too. It asks the local channel for at most a few
seconds, and reports a socket whose queue of connections is full as stuck. The
directory is closed to every account but its owner and root, so from an
ordinary account the owner of the file is reported as not readable; run
.B "sudo observer doctor"
to see it. The ownership check asks the system who owns the two paths and never
opens the store. Requires nothing, and it is the command that explains the
others when they fail.
.TP
.BI "token set " NAME
Keep the token of another machine on this computer, under the
.I NAME
that machine has in
.IR machines.json ,
so that it never has to be written into that file. The token is read from
standard input, never from the command line, and is not echoed while it is
typed.
.I NAME
may contain letters, digits, space, dot, underscore and hyphen, and nothing
else, because it becomes a file name. Requires nothing.
.TP
.BI "token forget " NAME
Remove that machine's token from this computer.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B share ,
.B rotate\-key
and
.B doctor
run as root inside
.IR /etc/observer ,
a folder that belongs to the
.B observer
account the service runs as. That account faces the network, so root does not
follow, wait on or read what it finds there in place of the store or of
.IR certificate.pfx :
a symbolic link at the name of the file, a FIFO, a device, a directory or a
socket, a file of more than 64 KiB, or a file with a second name that belongs to
another account than the folder. The command says which it found, says that
nothing was changed, and does not print what the file holds. A link or a second
name you made yourself is refused too: read what it points at with
.B "sudo cat"
instead.
.PP
Anything else is for you to judge. If you did not put the file there, stop the
service, remove it and start the service again. The service then creates a
.I new
token, and every dashboard that holds the old one stops connecting until it is
given the new one; for the certificate, the fingerprint changes and
.B share
shows the new one. A refusal that says nothing is known to be wrong with the
file means that this machine could not check it: do not delete it.
.B rotate\-key \-\-now
does not need what the old store holds, so a link or a FIFO at that name, a
file that cannot be parsed and one that holds something that is not a token do
not stop it: it says that the old store was not read and replaces the name,
and leaves what a link pointed to alone. An access denied still stops it. The
plain
.B rotate\-key
carries the old key over, so it refuses all of these.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. For
.B rotate\-key \-\-now
it means more than that, and it is the only thing it means: the old key is
provably accepted nowhere on this machine \- either because the running service
was seen to adopt the new store, or because nothing answered on the default
local channel and the default port.
.TP
.B 1
The credential store or the token store could not be read or written, or no
token was given. For
.B rotate\-key \-\-now
it also covers the case where the store WAS rewritten but the running service
was not seen to adopt it: it answers but has no such endpoint, it read a
different file, it has no store to adopt, it refused, or nothing answered on the
local channel while something is still listening on the port. The line printed
says which, and in every one of them the old key may still be accepted.
.TP
.B 2
The command was not recognised.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/observer/credentials.json
The credential store. Mode 0600, owned by the
.B observer
system user.
.TP
.I /run/observer/observer.sock
The local channel. Callers that are local and identifiable are served without
any token.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/Observer/secrets/NAME
The token of the machine called
.IR NAME ,
one file each, readable only by its owner. The dashboard refuses to use one
that group or others can read, or whose directory others can write to.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR observer-dashboard (1),
.BR systemctl (1)
